\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Complete orthogonal real isotypic projectors}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{Let #{G} be finite and let #{\rho} be an orthogonal action on a
finite-dimensional real inner-product space #{H}. Let #{Q} be a
linear operator commuting with every #{\rho(g)}.
Write #{\operatorname{Irr}_{\mathbb C}(G)} for the irreducible
complex [characters](fgap-001M). For each orbit #{\Xi} under
#{\chi\mapsto\overline\chi}, put
##{\begin{aligned}
e_\chi&=\frac{\chi(1)}{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\chi(g^{-1})[g],\\
\epsilon_\Xi&=\sum_{\chi\in\Xi}e_\chi,
& P_\Xi&=\widehat\rho(\epsilon_\Xi).
\end{aligned}}
Then #{\epsilon_\Xi} belongs to #{\mathbb R[G]}, is central,
and is fixed by the real involution #{[g]^*=[g^{-1}]}.
The operators satisfy
##{\begin{aligned}
P_\Xi^2&=P_\Xi=P_\Xi^*,& QP_\Xi&=P_\Xi Q,\\
P_\Xi P_\Upsilon&=0\quad(\Xi\ne\Upsilon),&
\sum_\Xi P_\Xi&=I_H.
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{H} is the orthogonal direct sum of their ranges; a range may
be zero. These ranges are its \newvocab{real isotypic sectors}:
each is the sum of all irreducible real subrepresentations of one
isomorphism type. Complexification of a sector is the sum of the
complex isotypic components with characters in #{\Xi}.}

\proof{
\p{Maschke's theorem and the
[complex central-idempotent formula](fgap-001N) give central,
pairwise orthogonal idempotents #{e_\chi} acting on the indicated
complex components. Coefficient conjugation sends #{e_\chi} to
#{e_{\overline\chi}}, so it fixes #{\epsilon_\Xi}; its coefficients
are real. Average a Hermitian inner product on a complex
representation over #{G}. Each group operator becomes unitary, whence
#{\chi(g^{-1})=\overline{\chi(g)}}. The complex linear extension of
#{[g]\mapsto[g^{-1}]} therefore sends #{e_\chi} to
#{e_{\overline\chi}}, and fixes #{\epsilon_\Xi}.
This is coefficient conjugation and inversion separately; the latter
restricts to the stated real involution.}

\p{For completeness, the regular character is
#{r_G(g)=|G|\,\delta_{g,1}} and decomposes as
#{r_G=\sum_\chi\chi(1)\chi} by
\citet{Section 2.4, Proposition 5 and Corollary 1, p. 18}{serre1977linear}.
Consequently
##{\begin{aligned}
\sum_\chi e_\chi
 &=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}
     \left(\sum_\chi\chi(1)\chi(g^{-1})\right)[g]\\
 &=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}r_G(g^{-1})[g]=[1].
\end{aligned}}
Grouping by conjugation orbits preserves this sum. Applying
#{\widehat\rho} gives idempotence, mixed-product zero and sum #{I_H}.
Orthogonality gives #{\rho(g)^*=\rho(g^{-1})}, so
#{\widehat\rho(a^*)=\widehat\rho(a)^*} for real coefficients.
Thus each #{P_\Xi} is self-adjoint; distinct ranges are perpendicular
because #{\langle P_\Xi x,P_\Upsilon y\rangle
=\langle x,P_\Xi P_\Upsilon y\rangle=0}.
The sum identity gives existence of the decomposition, and applying
each projector gives its uniqueness. Commutation with #{Q} follows
by linearity from commutation with every group operator.}

\p{To justify the real isotypic terminology, decompose #{H} into
irreducible real subrepresentations by Maschke. On any such #{U},
the real commuting idempotents #{P_\Xi} have invariant ranges, so
exactly one acts as the identity and the rest as zero.
Its complexification therefore has constituents in just that orbit.
Two real irreducibles assigned to the same orbit share a complex
constituent: complex conjugation on each complexification exchanges
the constituents indexed by #{\chi} and #{\overline\chi}.
Semisimplicity over #{\mathbb C} then gives a nonzero complex
intertwiner between their complexifications. In real bases, the real
and imaginary parts of its matrix are real intertwiners, at least
one nonzero. Its kernel and image are invariant, so irreducibility
makes it an isomorphism. Conversely an isomorphism commutes with all
the character projectors. This proves precisely the asserted grouping.}
}

\p{Serre's \citet{Section 2.6, Theorem 8, p. 21}{serre1977linear}
gives the canonical complex components and their projectors; the
real grouping and adjoint argument above are deductions from it.
TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Representation.sum_primitiveCentralIdempotent}
proves the complex sum-to-one identity for a finite pairwise inequivalent
family of irreducibles whose cardinality equals the number of conjugacy
classes, over an algebraically closed field with invertible group order.
It supports the completeness step, not the real adjoint or graph claims.}
